A||||9||JohnnyK||Radiator repair||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||08-03-2001||04:45 PM||||I have a hairline crack in my radiator about 1 inch long (this is a 95 honda accord by the way). I noticed this because my temp pegged so I popped the hood and saw a little hiss coming out of it with fluid leaking slowly down (a few drips at a time, but it would add up). I put in some prestone stop leak, and ran it for awhile, but that didn't seem to help. Is there stuff I can buy to put on the outside of the radiator? I didn't see any at the store. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||08-03-2001||04:51 PM||||I assume your leak is in the top tank. Isnt that plastic on Honda. You may be able to drain some out and clean the area good to get off any anti freeze. then use a dremel or drill to make a 'V' notch along the crack and apply some epoxy or JB Weld. Let it dry overnite and refill it. check it hot to see if its fixed before you get far away from home. Dont use any of those add in chemicals, they generally mess up things with aluminum parts....even if it says safe for aluminum. Youd just end up then having to get new core, new heater core and having the block/heads flushed. if the epoxy dont stop the leak, your gonna have to get a new core.<P>BTW if the leak is where the core and tank join its not repairable. they come as a unit.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 08-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||08-03-2001||05:30 PM||||Nah, it's just in the top tube on the radiator, right beside the radiator cap. I suppose I can't just replace that top tube, eh?  If the epoxy doesn't stop the leak, I'm going to have to get a new core? Whys that?<p>[This message has been edited by JohnnyK (edited 08-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||maryjane||08-03-2001||06:25 PM||||don't know if you saw this a month ago or so: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/Forum2/HTML/012121.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/Forum2/HTML/012121.html</A> <BR>or do a search- subject- plastic rad repair<BR>Don<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 08-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||08-04-2001||02:20 PM||||I mean "If the epoxy doesn't stop the leak your going to have to get a new core". I just want to know why before I go ahead and try anything.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||08-05-2001||10:11 AM||||theres no other way to fix plastic except epoxy. If its the top tank, like i said its attached to core by the manufacturer. they use a machine to bend over the bottom edges and shrink it tite around the metal core. Maybe some radiator shops can replace it but it beyond capabilities of most home mechanic.||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnnyK||08-05-2001||05:49 PM||||Oh yeah. Ok, I thought you were saying that if I use epoxy and it doesn't work, I'm going to need a new heater core because the epoxy got into the coolant or something like that. didn't know what you were saying. So worst case scenario is I have to buy a new radiator, which I'm going to have to do anyways if I doesn't work? THanks||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-05-2001||05:59 PM||||You have to be careful what you use for plastic tank repair. Not all epoxies will take the sustaned heat and pressure along with the chemicals in the coolant.<P>Unfortunatly, cracked Aluminum is the same way. the thin oxide layer that keeps Aluminum from corroding quickly is a problem for many adhesives. You can't sand away the layer. It forms instantly as new Al is exposed. People who weld/braze Al have the same problem.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||08-05-2001||06:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>Oh yeah. Ok, I thought you were saying that if I use epoxy and it doesn't work, I'm going to need a new heater core because the epoxy got into the coolant or something like that.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I think you got mixed up where I said that the stop leak stuff can clog up the engine or head water water passages or the heater core. dont use any stop leak. if you already did, flush it all out clean with the heater on full<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||JohnnyK||08-06-2001||02:47 PM||||Yeah, I'm gonna do that today (if I ever get around to it)..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
